WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:03.229
Then we've got the widget component.

2
00:00:03.229 --> 00:00:07.830
We've talked about creating these elements, how to display them on screen,

3
00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:15.230
but a relatively common, more modern setup to do is maybe we want these widgets to exist within world geometry

4
00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:20.629
or to respond to world space coordinates in some way, and that's where the widget component comes in.

5
00:00:20.629 --> 00:00:27.350
The widget component manifests 3D UI elements created through EMG in the world.

6
00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:32.549
The widget component itself is a 3D instance of the widget blueprint that you can interact with,

7
00:00:32.549 --> 00:00:36.549
and it offers a huge range of properties that we can leverage as developers,

8
00:00:36.549 --> 00:00:41.149
but some of the key properties we should be concerned about are the space.

9
00:00:41.149 --> 00:00:47.149
As the name implies, this is a component.

10
00:00:47.149 --> 00:00:49.149
It's a component you can put on actors.

11
00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:51.549
Those actors can be in the world, obviously.

12
00:00:51.549 --> 00:00:54.549
So then the space becomes quite important

13
00:00:54.549 --> 00:00:58.549
because this is the coordinate space in which this widget is rendered.

14
00:00:58.549 --> 00:01:01.549
So this can either be world or screen.

15
00:01:01.549 --> 00:01:08.549
World space means that the component's position, rotation, scale all get taken into account,

16
00:01:08.549 --> 00:01:16.549
and this widget is literally rendered as no different from a plane in your world, but with the widget on it.

17
00:01:16.549 --> 00:01:22.549
So it has perspective, it has the rotation matters, all of these things.

18
00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:28.549
Screen space is really the only property that matters about this component is its position.

19
00:01:28.549 --> 00:01:31.549
Its rotation doesn't matter, its scale doesn't matter,

20
00:01:31.549 --> 00:01:35.549
but its position is essentially projected onto your screen

21
00:01:35.549 --> 00:01:39.549
and is used for where this widget should be displayed on your screen.

22
00:01:39.549 --> 00:01:42.549
If you think about something like objective markers in other games,

23
00:01:42.549 --> 00:01:47.579
typically those things don't often tend to get a huge amount bigger,

24
00:01:47.579 --> 00:01:49.579
larger on your screen as you get closer to them.

25
00:01:49.579 --> 00:01:51.579
They don't get smaller as you get further away.

26
00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:56.579
They can stay the same size and they just indicate to you this thing could be two miles away,

27
00:01:56.579 --> 00:02:01.579
but they indicate to you in relation to your screen now, where would this objective be.

28
00:02:01.579 --> 00:02:06.579
That's the kind of use case for something like a screen space widget.

29
00:02:06.579 --> 00:02:12.099
The widget class obviously specifies what kind of widget is this component actually displaying.

30
00:02:12.099 --> 00:02:15.099
So that's a widget that we've probably created ourselves,

31
00:02:15.099 --> 00:02:18.099
inherited from the user widget class.

32
00:02:18.099 --> 00:02:26.479
The draw size, which is essentially the size of the quad that we're going to draw this widget onto.

33
00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:28.479
Whether or not the widget is focusable.

34
00:02:28.479 --> 00:02:35.479
So this determines whether or not the virtual window that gets created for this widget is focusable

35
00:02:35.479 --> 00:02:38.479
and should this take focus from the user.

36
00:02:38.479 --> 00:02:44.090
And then the pivot, which is a little bit like our alignment that we've talked about with the canvas panel,

37
00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:48.090
which is basically the ability to have where should the widget,

38
00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:51.090
how should the widget position itself in relation to the component.

39
00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:54.090
So if our pivot is 0.5, 0.5,

40
00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:58.090
the center of our widget is going to be aligned over the components position.

41
00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:02.090
If our pivot is 0.51,

42
00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:11.090
then the center bottom of the component is going to be where the widget is going to be where the component actually is.

43
00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:16.090
So where the pivot of the widget in relation to the component itself.
